{} [:a {:href "/venues/874"} "Olympic Stadium, Olympic Park, Stratford, London"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Participants"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/events/302089"} "79 from 65 countries"]]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "The race was considered to be between the two Jamaicans, " [:a {:href "/athletes/114740"} "Shelly-Ann Fraser-Pryce"] ", the defending champion as Shelly-Ann Fraser, and " [:a {:href "/athletes/87116"} "Veronica Campbell-Brown"] ", Olympic champion in 2004 and 2008 at 200 metres, and American, " [:a {:href "/athletes/126627"} "Carmelita Jeter"] ", the 2011 World Champion at 100 metres. American " [:a {:href "/athletes/105293"} "Allyson Felix"] " was an outside shot for a medal but she was far better known as a 200 runner."] "\n\n" [:p {} "The times were quick in the heats and semi as the Olympic Stadium’s Mondo surface lived up to its reputation as a fast track. All four favorites made it to the final, along with the third American, " [:a {:href "/athletes/126527"} "Tianna Madison"] ". Fraser-Pryce had the best start, but Jeter was just behind. By 50 metres those two were running even, but in the final 20 metres, Fraser-Pryce leaned forward as she edged out Jeter by 3/100ths, 10.75 to 10.78. Campbell-Brown completed the podium in third, with Madison fourth, and Felix fifth, although they both set PRs. The race was fast as the best-ever marks-for-place were recorded in 3rd, 4th, 6th, 7th, and 8th."] "\n\n" [:p {} "The victory made Fraser-Pryce only the third woman to defend her Olympic 100 crown, after " [:a {:href "/athletes/77993"} "Wyomia Tyus"] " in 1964-68 and " [:a {:href "/athletes/77805"} "Gail Devers"] " in 1992-96."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Number"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "Preliminary Round"] [:th {} "R1"] [:th {} "SF"] [:th {} "Final"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {:class "bib"} "2197"] [:td {} [:a {:hre